Therefore, the expected TPOT improvement is bounded by the minimum of the total
compute time and the CPU→\rightarrowGPU copy time. The maximum achievable
speedup is 2×2\times, when compute and copy times are equal. In
practice, GPUs are optimized for high compute throughput,
and CPU→\rightarrowGPU transfers occur over relatively slower
interconnects such as PCIe. As a result, copy time typically dominates
(tcopy≫tcomputet_{\text{copy}}\gg t_{\text{compute}}), limiting the achievable speedup.




